---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import SeedWarning from '../../components/SeedWarning.astro';
import FigureRaceWindow from '../../components/figures/FigureRaceWindow.astro';

/**
 * This page absorbed /risk/migrating/. The two pages shared the same seven
 * ordered steps and spent several hundred words routing readers between each
 * other; the threshold-wallet material that genuinely differs is now the rung
 * at the foot, which a single-signature owner never has to open.
 */
---

<Article
  title="Moving funds from an affected seed"
  description="The ordered migration procedure: generate a replacement seed on fixed firmware or another trusted device, verify the backup and fingerprint, verify a receive address, test, move the funds, confirm, then retire the old words."
  standfirst="For a wallet built from affected words, the order of these steps is what keeps a migration from creating a second problem."
  depth="plain"
  kicker="Your risk"
>
  <Answer>
    <p>
      Generate a replacement seed on firmware or a device you trust, verify its
      backup, fingerprint and a receive address before anything is sent, move a
      test amount, then move the rest and wait for confirmations before treating the old
      words as finished. Nothing in the procedure requires disclosing recovery
      material to anybody. If the wallet is a threshold wallet whose script or
      descriptor has never been published, the first spend reveals it, so read
      the rung at the foot of this page before signing.
    </p>
  </Answer>

  <h2>
    The procedure, in order
    <Claim
      basis="derived"
      scope="The ordering of the seven steps above and the reasons given for each one"
      source="Derived from the affected generation path, the wallet properties documented on the risk pages, and the standard-derivation search this incident describes; no device-specific menu path or vendor procedure is asserted"
      href="/risk/"
      compact
    />
  </h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Generate a replacement seed on a device you can trust.</strong>
      Use fixed firmware, or a different, independently assessed signing device.
      <Claim
        basis="unverified"
        scope="That the vendor-published hotfix binaries provide the intended entropy at runtime, which step one depends on"
        source="This project verified the source-level fix and the release record but has not reproduced, rebuilt or disassembled the signed images"
        href="/record/firmware/"
        compact
      />
      The replacement words must be generated fresh: do not re-derive them from
      the affected words, do not reuse any part of them, and do not treat a
      passphrase added to the affected mnemonic as a new seed.
      <a href="/record/firmware/#check-your-device">How to check your device's
      firmware version</a> covers establishing what is actually running before
      you generate anything.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Verify the new backup and the master fingerprint before anything
      is sent.</strong> Record the new words, then check the record back against
      the device rather than assuming the transcription is correct. Confirm that
      the master fingerprint the device reports is the fingerprint your wallet
      software shows for the account you are about to use. A backup that has not
      been read back, and a wallet whose fingerprint has not been matched, are
      not yet a destination.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Verify a receive address on the trusted device's own display.</strong>
      Read the address from the new device and compare it against the address
      the sending software shows. Compromised sending software can substitute a
      destination address; checking the signer's own display tests for that
      specific failure before funds are sent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Send a small test amount first, and confirm it.</strong> Send an
      amount you can afford to lose, wait for it to confirm, and check that it
      appears in the new wallet under the fingerprint you verified. Choose the
      confirmation and wallet-check threshold before starting. The old outputs
      remain exposed during any delay between meeting that threshold and sending
      the remainder.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Move the remainder.</strong> Send the remaining outputs to the
      new wallet. Where you can, broadcast through a path that has not handled
      the affected seed's private keys, for example your own node or a wallet
      that holds only public data. Sign with replaceability enabled where your
      software supports it, so an underestimated fee can be bumped rather than
      leaving the transaction unconfirmed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wait for confirmations, then inventory before retiring
      anything.</strong> Confirm that the move is buried, not merely relayed.
      Before the old seed is treated as finished, work out what else was derived
      from it: BIP85 children, seed XOR shares, device-generated paper wallets,
      encrypted backups and any second wallet built on the same words all
      inherit its generation history. <a href="/how-it-broke/blast-radius/">What
      else is affected</a> lists the call sites and states what each one does and
      does not settle.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retire the old backup so it cannot be mistaken for live.</strong>
      Once nothing of value derives from the old words, mark or destroy the
      backup according to how you store it. A retired backup left indistinguishable
      from a current one is how an affected wallet gets restored months later
      and funds sent back to it. Never reuse the affected words for anything, including a
      low-value or decoy wallet: the search runs over candidate seeds rather than
      over particular wallets, so a small balance controlled by those words is found by the
      same work.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <span class="callout__title">Reported ordering: move the funds before upgrading the affected device</span>
    <p style="margin:0">
      <a href="/record/sources/lopp-migrate-before-upgrade/">Jameson Lopp advised on 2 August</a>
      moving funds off a weakly generated seed before upgrading the device's
      firmware, citing reports of a non-zero chance that the upgrade bricks
      the device.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="Lopp's guidance to migrate funds off a weakly generated seed before upgrading firmware, and his citation of reported bricking risk"
        source="Jameson Lopp, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/lopp-migrate-before-upgrade/"
        compact
      />
      <a href="/record/sources/bitcoinnews-bricking-warning/">Bitcoin News reported the same day</a>,
      secondhand, that users were saying the new upgrade was bricking some
      devices, with the same move-first advice.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The secondhand report that the new firmware upgrade was bricking some devices"
        source="Bitcoin News, captured X post, 2 Aug 2026; no first-hand account or device count is cited there, and none is held here as of 3 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/bitcoinnews-bricking-warning/"
        compact
      />
      No first-hand bricking account
      is captured in this archive as of 3 August 2026, and neither post cites
      a device count. The reports bear on step one: they argue for generating
      the replacement wallet and completing the move before the affected
      device itself is updated, rather than updating it first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <details class="collapsible">
    <summary>Why steps one and five are worded that way</summary>
    <div class="collapsible__body">
      <p style="margin-bottom:11px">
        Installing the hotfix does not repair words that already exist: it
        changes what the device generates next. If you would rather not depend on
        the device's generator at all, the pure-dice path starts from the roll
        string and bypasses it, subject to the roll minimums on
        <a href="/risk/mitigations/#dice">the dice rung</a>.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The broadcast advice in step five is about environments, not privacy in
        general: if the affected words were ever typed into a recovery tool or a
        general-purpose computer, that environment should not also be the one
        that learns about the replacement wallet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <SeedWarning compact />

  <p class="cap">
    A transaction-submission service needs a signed transaction, not the material
    used to sign it. <a href="/safety/seed-words/">Seed-word safety</a> covers
    what to do if recovery material has already been disclosed, and
    <a href="/safety/scams/">scams and impersonation</a> lists the attempts this
    archive has recorded.
  </p>

  <h2>Fees, and what waiting actually costs</h2>

  <p>
    A migration is an ordinary transaction and pays an ordinary fee. Waiting for
    a lower-fee period may reduce that cost, but it leaves the affected output in
    place. The first observed wave spent outputs from 500 source addresses inside
    four consecutive blocks, 960,188 to 960,191, which does not establish a
    deadline for another wallet.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The 500-address, four-block characterisation of the first observed wave used above"
      source="Reported by Rob Hamilton and TFTC; the block span is re-checkable on any public block explorer"
      href="/record/funds/"
      compact
    />
    If the fee is a large fraction of a balance,
    moving it may cost more than the amount moved, so balance, current fee,
    confidence in the exposure assessment and uncertainty in the published search
    models are separate inputs to the choice.
    <Claim
      basis="unverified"
      scope="Whether any particular wallet is being searched now, and how long an exposed single-signature output can be expected to survive"
      source="No held evidence identifies targeting of a specific wallet, and the published candidate-space estimates differ by many orders of magnitude, so no deadline is implied here"
      href="/how-it-broke/entropy/"
      compact
    />
  </p>

  <p>
    Preparing and verifying the destination creates no on-chain transaction and
    can be completed before the fee decision. Moving only some outputs reduces
    exposure only for those outputs and can have fee and privacy consequences. An
    unresolved firmware or provenance question is not the same as a negative
    answer: <a href="/risk/">the risk overview</a> sets out which facts settle it
    without touching recovery material.
  </p>

  <Disclosure
    id="threshold-wallets"
    title="Threshold wallets: the first spend can publish the script"
    note="only if a spending path has at least as many affected keys as its threshold, and the script is still hidden"
  >
    <p>
      This applies to a narrow case: a threshold wallet has at least as many
      affected keys as a spending-path threshold, and its relevant script,
      descriptor and child public keys have not already been disclosed. In that
      case the first spend can make individual weak-key tests possible before the
      transaction confirms. Whether the case applies is a technical condition
      rather than a matter of address reuse, and it is worked through on
      <a href="/risk/mitigations/#multisig">the multisig rung</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>What becomes public during a spend</h3>

    <p>
      A P2WSH output contains a hash of the witness script, and a valid spend
      supplies that script and the required signatures. When an unconfirmed
      transaction is publicly relayed, peers that receive it can inspect the
      script and its child public keys before a block confirms the spend. An
      attacker who can recover a threshold number of keys within that interval
      may attempt a conflicting spend with a more attractive fee. The same
      disclosure occurs for nested P2SH-P2WSH: its <code>scriptSig</code>
      supplies the redeem-script witness program while the witness supplies the
      complete witness script and signatures. Because the published
      candidate-space estimates vary by many orders of magnitude, this mechanism
      does not establish that every such race is practical; it identifies when a
      hidden-script assumption ends.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The first-spend race and direct-submission recommendation described above"
        source="Rob Hamilton, Peter Todd and Charles Guillemet described the first-spend race; the native and nested P2WSH reveal mechanics are verified from the transaction structure"
        href="/record/sources/robhamilton-multisig-psa/"
        compact
      />
    </p>

    <FigureRaceWindow />

    <h3>Submission options and limitations</h3>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table">
          <caption>Migration transaction routes</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="nw">Option</th>
              <th scope="col" class="wide">Pre-confirmation exposure</th>
              <th scope="col" class="wide">Limitations to check</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong>Public relay with a competitive fee</strong></td>
              <td class="wide">The script is visible to peers from first relay until confirmation.</td>
              <td class="wide">A next-block fee is a target, not a confirmation guarantee. Block intervals are variable, policy differs across miners, and a conflicting transaction may pay a higher fee.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong>Direct submission to a miner</strong></td>
              <td class="wide">Disclosure can be limited to the service and its participating infrastructure if neither the service nor another participant relays the transaction elsewhere.</td>
              <td class="wide">Confirmation depends on the service accepting the transaction and a participating miner finding a suitable block. Review confidentiality, retention, fee, replacement and non-confirmation terms. Do not assume a guarantee that is not written.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nw"><strong>Taproot key-path spend</strong></td>
              <td class="wide">The aggregate output key is already visible in the output being spent. A key-path spend adds a signature but does not reveal an unused script leaf.</td>
              <td class="wide">This is available only if the wallet was constructed with an executable key path and the required signers and software can use it. It cannot be added retroactively to a P2WSH output.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      <a href="/record/sources/guillemet-multisig-relay-analysis/">Charles Guillemet</a>
      described the complete-script condition, the possibility that copies already
      exist in provider systems or backups, and the public-mempool conflict
      window, recommending a trusted private relay and naming Slipstream as one
      example.
    </p>

    <h3>What is known about Slipstream</h3>

    <p>
      MARA describes Slipstream as a direct transaction-submission service backed
      by MARA Pool. At the 1 August 2026 check, its portal required a client code
      and applied a minimum submission feerate. MARA's public material supports
      describing it as direct submission, but the portal terms do not establish a
      confidentiality or confirmation guarantee.
      <Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="The public Slipstream submission process and limitations described above"
        source="MARA Slipstream portal, checked 1 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/mara-slipstream-portal/"
        compact
      />
      The separately published
      <a href="/record/sources/mara-slipstream-api/">OpenAPI document</a>
      describes transaction handling as best effort and says admission does not
      guarantee mining; its request schema marks <code>client_code</code> as
      optional, while the portal says client codes are currently required.
      <Claim
        basis="verified"
        contested
        scope="The differing public client-code descriptions and the API's best-effort, no-mining-guarantee language"
        source="MARA Slipstream portal and OpenAPI document, checked 1 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/mara-slipstream-api/"
        compact
      />
      This
      archive did not submit a transaction to test which condition the service
      enforced on 1 August 2026.
    </p>

    <p>
      A dated pool-share observation gives scale to that limitation. In
      a <a href="/record/evidence/mempool-pools-1m-20260801T055613Z.json">held copy of mempool.space's rolling one-month pool dataset</a>,
      retrieved at 05:56 UTC on 1 August, MARA Pool had found 197 of 4,343
      blocks in the response, or 4.54%. The total includes 44 blocks in the
      endpoint's Unknown category. If that share stays constant and network blocks
      arrive every ten minutes on average, the implied mean interval between MARA
      blocks is about 220 minutes, or 3.7 hours.
      <Claim
        basis="derived"
        scope="The 4.54% observed MARA Pool share and 3.7-hour mean-block-interval calculation"
        source="Held mempool.space one-month response at 2026-08-01T05:56:13Z, file SHA-256 906d774288ef2e5fee016e90b44c462af835a4932da52c6581975f700b5fb0b7; mean = 10 minutes divided by 197/4,343"
        href="/record/evidence/mempool-pools-1m-20260801T055613Z.json"
        compact
      />
      Pool share and block intervals
      vary, and acceptance, fee policy, conflicts and service behaviour remain
      separate conditions.
    </p>

    <h3>A time-stamped risk assessment</h3>

    <p>
      <a href="https://x.com/LLFOURN/status/2083375420721025313" rel="noopener nofollow">LLFOURN assessed the immediate risk of moving Mk4 multisig as low</a> on 1
      August, while warning that the same assessment might not hold a few days
      later.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="LLFOURN's time-specific assessment of the migration race"
        source="LLFOURN, migration-risk assessment published 1 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/llfourn-2083375420721025313/"
        compact
      />
      The post describes <a href="https://x.com/unchained/status/2083036112449163618" rel="noopener nofollow">Unchained's cautious, one-size process</a>
      as sensible for a KYC provider that knows its customer base, and notes that
      waiting means trusting the provider's eventual solution. The date is part of
      the claim.
    </p>

    <h3>Provenance of the recommendation</h3>

    <p>
      <a href="/record/sources/robhamilton-multisig-psa/">Rob Hamilton</a>,
      an AnchorWatch co-founder, published the migration warning.
      <a href="/record/sources/petertodd-slipstream-guidance/">Peter Todd</a>
      subsequently endorsed direct miner submission as a possible way to reduce
      the public-mempool interval. <a href="/record/sources/portlandhodl-slipstream-access/">PortlandHODL</a>, who has described
      involvement in building Slipstream and lists an AnchorWatch affiliation,
      offered access codes during the incident. Charles Guillemet later published
      a similar recommendation while serving as Ledger's CTO, and Ledger sells
      competing signing devices.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The disclosed employment and authorship connections behind the Slipstream recommendation"
        source="Captured X posts and public profiles; no inference about motive is made"
        href="/record/sources/portlandhodl-slipstream-access/"
        compact
      />
      These relationships are disclosed because they
      connect the advice either to the service or to a competing hardware-wallet
      vendor. They do not establish that the technical mechanism is incorrect.
    </p>

    <h3>A reported migration outcome</h3>

    <p>
      <a href="/record/sources/portlandhodl-slipstream-outcome/">PortlandHODL later reported</a>
      that a transaction submitted through Slipstream and included in the block
      current when he posted had saved approximately 158.6 BTC from a 2-of-3
      multisig wallet whose COLDCARD keys were likely exposed.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="PortlandHODL's approximately 158.6 BTC Slipstream outcome claim"
        source="PortlandHODL, captured primary X post, 1 Aug 2026; no transaction identifier or independent counterfactual evidence is held"
        href="/record/sources/portlandhodl-slipstream-outcome/"
        compact
      />
      The held post does
      not identify the transaction, wallet policy, competing-spend evidence or
      counterfactual outcome. This archive therefore records the statement as a
      reported service outcome and does not independently classify the funds as
      saved by Slipstream.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout--red">
      <span class="callout__title">Treat direct-message access as an authentication problem</span>
      Verify any service domain, representative and access code through a second
      published channel. Do not provide seed words, private keys, passphrases or
      wallet backups. A transaction-submission service needs the signed transaction,
      not the material used to sign it.
    </div>

    <h3>Before signing or submitting</h3>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Confirm the case.</strong> Identify the spending path, threshold, affected-key count and whether the script, descriptor or child public keys are already exposed.</li>
      <li><strong>Assess the candidate-space assumptions.</strong> Do not treat a lower or upper published scenario as a measured recovery time for the wallet.</li>
      <li><strong>Review the submission route.</strong> Check current service terms, fees, replacement behaviour, data handling and what happens if no block is found promptly.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid leaving a residual unintentionally.</strong> Any spent script-path input reveals its witness script. Confirm which outputs and derivation paths remain after the planned transaction.</li>
    </ol>
  </Disclosure>
</Article>
